Environmental considerations also shape purchasing choices; spirulina’s water use efficiency-requiring roughly 10 % of the resources needed for conventional livestock protein-aligns with corporate sustainability targets and draws eco conscious buyers.
Market Challenges
Energy Intensive Drying Processes
Drying accounts for up to 38 % of total production costs, creating a cost ceiling for manufacturers that rely on open pond systems. Facilities that have transitioned to low temperature vacuum drying report a 12 % reduction in energy spend, yet the capital outlay remains a barrier for smaller growers.
Consumer Taste Perception
Surveys indicate that 27 % of potential buyers associate spirulina with an unpleasant flavor, limiting its inclusion in mainstream food products. Companies counter this by masking the taste with natural fruit extracts and by promoting powdered formats where the algae blends seamlessly.
Emerging Opportunities
Clinical trials published in 2025 demonstrate spirulina derived phycocyanin’s potential to modulate immune response in viral infections, opening a therapeutic segment projected to generate USD 115 million by 2028. Parallel research on antioxidant complexes has spurred premium skincare lines that tout anti aging benefits, prompting a 22 % year over year rise in spirulina based cosmetic formulations.
